    "text": "To find the integer whose square is less than its double, we can set up an inequality:\n\nx^2 < 2x\n\nNow, we can rearrange the inequality:\n\nx^2 - 2x < 0\n\nNext, we can factor the left side of the inequality:\n\nx(x - 2) < 0\n\nNow, we can find the critical points by setting each factor equal to zero:\n\nx = 0\nx - 2 = 0\n\nThe critical points are x = 0 and x = 2. We can now test the intervals between these critical points to determine where the inequality holds true:\n\n1. For x < 0, both factors are negative, so their product is positive. This does not satisfy the inequality.\n\n2. For 0 < x < 2, the first factor is positive and the second factor is negative, so their product is negative. This satisfies the inequality.\n\n3. For x > 2, both factors are positive, so their product is positive. This does not satisfy the inequality.\n\nTherefore, the only integer whose square is less than its double is x = 1. The final answer is:\n\n\\boxed{1}",
